WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.650
- Hello, and welcome to "Ed
Talks with Khan Academy".

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.940
Thank you for joining us today.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:37.773
I'm Kristen DiCerbo,

00:00:37.773 --> 00:00:40.110
the Chief Learning Officer at Khan Academy

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.300
and I'm excited today to
talk with Nick Ferroni

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.770
who's going to talk about
what would it look like

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.260
if we really appreciated teachers?

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.990
I'm excited to get on with that.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.240
Before we get started a couple reminders.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.250
First, at Khan Academy, we are a nonprofit

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.140
and we rely on donations
from folks like you

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.330
to keep doing the work we are.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.830
If you go to khanacademy.org/donate,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.180
you will find a place where
you can easily contribute

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.023
and help us continue to
work towards our mission.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.590
Second, we wanna thank
some of the supporters

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.270
who have helped us address
the COVID-19 crisis,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.543
including AT&amp;T, General Motors and Fastly.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.840
And finally, if you are
interested in listening

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.170
to this discussion or any
other discussions that I've had

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.387
or Sal has had with some of our

00:01:29.387 --> 00:01:31.260
"Homeroom" and "Ed Talk" guests,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.410
you can go to "Homeroom with Sal"

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:36.070
at wherever you get your podcasts

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.543
and hear audio versions of these chats.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.610
So with that, let me
welcome Mr. Nick Ferroni.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.930
Nick, thanks for joining us today.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.490
- Thank you so much for having me, Kris.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.550
I'm just finishing my
school day currently.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.210
- Yes, so let me tell folks
a little bit about you.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.580
You are a high school
teacher who teaches math

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.580
and cultural studies and mentor
a diverse group of students,

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.410
including founding your school's GSA,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.120
Gay Straight Alliance and Feminist Club.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.600
And I'll brag on you a little bit.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.370
Nick was named one of
100 Making A Difference

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.660
for his commitment to education reform,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.460
and has also recently

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.657
been named as...

00:02:20.657 --> 00:02:22.630
Oh, I lost my place there.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.930
As well as recently named
the Upstander of the Year

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.400
by the Human Rights Campaign.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.620
I like that title, Upstander of the Year,

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.460
for your advocacy and
support of LGBTQ youth.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.840
So welcome and it is a
pleasure to talk to you today.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.120
- Well, thank you so much.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.260
And history and cultural studies
are definitely my expertise

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.240
but thank you, thank you
so much for having me.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.073
It's so great to be here.

00:02:43.073 --> 00:02:45.760
I've been looking forward to this so much.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.950
- Great, so I like to
start by asking people

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:50.335
about their career path.

00:02:50.335 --> 00:02:52.852
Did you always wanna be a teacher?

00:02:52.852 --> 00:02:55.490
How did you come to be
where you are today?

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.590
- Wow, that is such an
interesting question.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.030
I always joke around and
say as a kid I wanted to be

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.190
a superhero, a philosopher,
a philanthropist,

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.150
a comedian, an actor, and an adventurer,

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.850
so I became a teacher.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.628
It's kind of like-
- There you go.

00:03:09.628 --> 00:03:11.880
- (indistinct) school
all encompassed in one.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.310
I had amazing educators growing up.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.660
Teaching was not my major focus.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.900
I always kind of thought
I wanted to be a mentor,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.830
a coach in that field.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:27.400
I went to Rutgers to play
football and I majored in history.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.170
At that point, that's kind
of when I started to realize

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.780
maybe education and history was kind of

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.610
what I would wanna pursue.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.390
Indiana Jones was probably
one of my biggest influences.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:37.327
I'm like, "If I can do that,

00:03:37.327 --> 00:03:39.837
"if I could go on
adventures in the weekend

00:03:39.837 --> 00:03:41.690
"and be a professor."

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.210
But it's after college, ironically enough,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.320
I ended up acting.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.210
I was actually waiting for a teaching job

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.420
to open up in my old high school,

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.930
which I had been teaching at
my alma mater now for 19 years,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.150
which is such an interesting
experience in itself.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.700
But I was waiting for a teaching job.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.557
My cousin's a big agent, so she was like,

00:04:01.557 --> 00:04:02.887
"Let me send you on some castings

00:04:02.887 --> 00:04:06.000
"and make you some money while
you're waiting to teach."

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.300
I ended up booking a few jobs,
I ended up on a soap opera,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.340
and I started to do that while
I was subbing in my school.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.380
So I ended up to work more and more.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.670
I mean, I wasn't a big time
player, I was a 10 and under

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.480
which meant I had a few
lines every so often.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.647
And eventually they
had me on more and more

00:04:20.647 --> 00:04:22.400
and I was getting more opportunities.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.260
And then a teaching job opened
up at my old high school.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.580
So I had to pick between
pursuing something

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.520
that I wasn't really passionate about

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.330
and I wasn't trained in,

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:32.690
I didn't think I would ever be good at,

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.700
I definitely had imposter syndrome

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.760
or become a high school teacher.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.660
The irony being that
I went from putting on

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.680
one show a day to six shows a day

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.240
to an audience that
does not wanna be there.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.890
I will say this with absolute certainty.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.900
Every great teacher
could be a great actor,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.230
not all great actors
could be great teachers

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.350
because our job is so performative

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.740
and it's so about engagement,

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.370
but I now find myself in my 19th year

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.330
teaching in my alma mater.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.510
It's the greatest job in the world.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.370
If teaching was my only job

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.400
it would be the easiest job in the world,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.220
but there are so many other facets.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.070
And so that's how I'm back
here now at Union High School.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.170
- I love that acting is the thing you do

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:15.870
until a teaching job opens up.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.680
That's (laughing) a
great way to think about

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.740
the potential hierarchy of
careers that we think about.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.720
So you said you had some great teachers

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.970
and it's Teacher Appreciation Week,

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:29.510
which we'll talk some more about,

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.420
but given that, do you
have a teacher or two

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.480
that you'd like to specifically point to

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.280
as influential for you?

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.350
- God, luckily I was very
fortunate to have so many.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.380
I mean, my history teachers growing up

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.070
just made me passionate about history.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.180
Passionate people make you passionate.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.720
But I would say whenever
I talk about anything,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.740
I always talk about Mrs.
Martino, my kindergarten teacher,

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.260
and she to me was the
epitome of educators.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.420
I came from a very Catholic upbringing,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:01.110
so being lefthanded,
my Italian grandmother

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.410
was not very happy about that.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.390
She would constantly try to
force me to be right-handed.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.460
And obviously as a kid
that's kind of traumatic

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.320
'cause you feel like
something's wrong with you.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.100
And then Mrs. Martino
was the one who got me

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.350
to embrace being lefthanded.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.710
She would read me books
about left-handed people.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.570
And the irony being I
always tell these stories

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.630
whenever I do any press.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.600
I do a lot of press for
obviously in reference to being

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.880
an educator and to advocate for teachers.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.900
I always tell the stories
about how Mrs. Martino,

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.700
I would have the toughest
days of my life as a student

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.510
coming in, and she would always
nurture me and read to me.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:34.260
And then one day in
particular, I always say how

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.250
I was having such a rough day
because my grandmother again

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.170
was forcing this kind of
on me to be right-handed

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.270
because left-handed was
sinister in her mindset.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.647
She brought me over to
the toy box and she said,

00:06:45.647 --> 00:06:47.520
"I want you to pick your favorite toy."

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.230
And of course being a kindergarten student

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.660
I reach in and grab the
He-Man action figure.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:53.493
She let me keep it.

00:06:53.493 --> 00:06:55.410
It was the greatest moment in my life.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.900
So I always tell the story
on how teachers are so good

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.280
at knowing what their
students need emotionally.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.510
The irony being Mrs. Martino
and I have kept in touch

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.270
on social media, on Facebook.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.070
So whenever I do a story, Kristen,

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:09.550
I'll always send her a link to it.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.770
I didn't see her since I was her student.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:12.930
So I always send her the links

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.350
and I always get the same response,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.667
"I am so proud of you, I love you so much,

00:07:16.667 --> 00:07:18.220
"say hi to your mom."

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.490
And finally I did "The Today
Show" and I told the story

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.520
about how my kindergarten
teacher, Mrs. Martino

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.180
let me keep this He-Man action figure

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.260
and it was such a simple act,
but it meant so much to me.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.897
And she replied back to me,

00:07:28.897 --> 00:07:30.697
"I love you so much, I'm so proud of you.

00:07:30.697 --> 00:07:32.847
"It means so much that
you think so much of me,

00:07:32.847 --> 00:07:34.840
"but I have to be honest with you."

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.820
So you'll know where this
story's going because

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.700
in the history of histories,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.200
I don't think a woman has ever
told a man in any situation

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.260
that she has to be honest with them

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:45.643
and something good has come out of it.

00:07:45.643 --> 00:07:46.810
(Kristen laughing)

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.910
I don't think you've ever sat
down your husband or spouse

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.240
or partner and said, "We need to talk,"

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.010
and it be something positive.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.970
So I messaged her back and I
was like, "Is everything okay?"

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.030
Of course, I mean about her.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.657
And she's like, "Well, I
need to be honest with you.

00:07:58.657 --> 00:08:01.070
"I just, I wanna clear something up."

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.030
And I am who I am
because of the confidence

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:06.660
that this woman built
when I was a young child.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.580
My mom's amazing, but
Mrs. Martino just added

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.500
that confidence and that empowerment.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.027
My thought was, "Is she gonna tell me

00:08:14.027 --> 00:08:15.577
"that I wasn't a talented kid

00:08:15.577 --> 00:08:17.407
"and I was annoying-"
- Oh, no.

00:08:17.407 --> 00:08:19.727
- "and I kind of shocked her?

00:08:19.727 --> 00:08:20.560
"Like, 'Nick, I have to be honest.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.117
"'You really blew me away.

00:08:22.117 --> 00:08:23.880
"'I thought you'd turned
out to be nothing.'"

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.610
I was waiting for that response.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:26.630
And so she's typing,

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.300
my heart's beating 100 miles an hour

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.100
because the woman who I
am who I am because of

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.380
is gonna crush me at any moment.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.950
So she's typing.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:36.590
And again, I'm in my 30s
when this is happening.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.520
So my kindergarten
teacher is gonna destroy

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.760
a 30 year old man's ego in a heartbeat.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.567
And finally her comment
comes up and she's like,

00:08:43.567 --> 00:08:45.537
"Nicholas, I love you so
much, I'm so proud of you,

00:08:45.537 --> 00:08:47.507
"but you keep telling people

00:08:47.507 --> 00:08:48.747
"I was your kindergarten teacher.

00:08:48.747 --> 00:08:50.087
"I was your preschool teacher.

00:08:50.087 --> 00:08:52.617
"And you keep saying it
was a He-man action figure

00:08:52.617 --> 00:08:54.900
"and it was a Fonzie doll."

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.537
And of course I go back and
I text my mom like, "Mom,

00:08:58.537 --> 00:09:00.970
"do you remember the toy that
Ms. Martino let me keep?"

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:02.327
And she's like, "What, that weird little

00:09:02.327 --> 00:09:04.210
"Fonzie action figure?"

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.270
And I was like, "It was a Fonzie doll."

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:11.270
So to me, 30 years later, this
woman remembered the moment,

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.490
the day, even the toy.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:19.290
I felt the only, only logical
response was to surprise her

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:20.970
and go see her.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.110
So that is a picture-
- Oh.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:25.710
- of me surprising Mrs.
Martino 30 years later.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.240
She opened the door, she started
crying, I started crying.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.300
It was pretty intense.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.250
The other thing I wanna point out

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:32.540
to elementary school teachers,

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.930
you are the most patient
people on the planet,

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:36.610
'cause you have to wait 30 years

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.453
to find out if you did a good job.

00:09:39.584 --> 00:09:42.450
So I get immediate gratification
being a high school teacher

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.790
but yes, please don't wait 30 years

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:46.680
to thank your elementary school teachers

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:48.650
on how amazing they were.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.120
But it's like that's one of the moments,

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.220
and it was just such an
amazing moment for me

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.870
because it's Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.120
It's never too early to thank a teacher

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.530
who impacted your life
but it can be too late.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.290
So I always encourage people
to not wait for that moment,

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:03.530
to take advantage of it.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.600
But yeah, Mrs. Martino to me

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.990
is who I try to be as an educator.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:10.530
Every student walked in
the classroom feeling loved

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.807
and she would always tell
me, and she'd always say,

00:10:13.807 --> 00:10:15.577
"They will forget what you teach 'em,

00:10:15.577 --> 00:10:17.677
"but they will never forget
how you made them feel

00:10:17.677 --> 00:10:19.540
"when they were in your
classroom, in your presence."

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.290
And that's something as a
teacher has become my mantra.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.267
- That was my takeaway, part of my

00:10:25.267 --> 00:10:27.040
one of my takeaways from that too

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:28.760
was you didn't remember the detail.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.230
You actually didn't even
remember which grade she taught,

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.980
but you remembered the way she
made you feel and so I think-

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.170
- I also might've suppressed-
- that's the example.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.150
- I might have also suppressed

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.720
the fact it was a Fonzie doll.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:39.670
He-Man's a lot cooler.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.640
Like even that fact,
(Kristen laughing)

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.480
that detail of the fact
that she remembered.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.850
But yeah, it's she remembered
those moments and I didn't

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.963
but it's I just remembered
how loved and how much I felt.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:52.693
- Yeah. Yep.

00:10:52.693 --> 00:10:55.460
And the confidence that it gave
you, that she gave you too.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:56.813
Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:10:57.949 --> 00:10:59.720
I love it, love the fantastic story.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.700
I'm gonna key in on one
other piece you mentioned

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.670
was reading books that made you feel like

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:06.860
you were seeing yourself in the book too,

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:10.195
which is another thing
that I feel is so important

00:11:10.195 --> 00:11:13.286
for folks to be able to see themselves

00:11:13.286 --> 00:11:17.930
and get that from books, I
feel like is such a gift.

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:19.610
- Well, children's books, I mean,

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:21.469
I don't think people realize
the impact they have,

00:11:21.469 --> 00:11:24.160
not only for children to see themselves,

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:26.520
but for other children
to see other children

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.980
and to see different
groups and see diversity.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:32.440
And it's like, I mean, it's
and she was so incredible

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.230
way before our time,

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.840
way before the inclusive
nature we live in,

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.760
she read books on every group, everything,

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:40.900
children with disabilities,
different races.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.947
And it was such a powerful
lesson, it's like...

00:11:42.947 --> 00:11:45.560
And I mean, I am the person I am

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:47.860
because of the empathy and
compassion and understanding

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.050
that was instilled on me at that time.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:51.460
And I think that's why it's so important

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.310
that educators are supporting that,

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:56.500
are providing inclusive books.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.032
And there are so many amazing
children's books out there,

00:12:00.032 --> 00:12:02.170
which everyone has seen.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.650
And the sad reality is I can
imagine that there may be

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.940
some classrooms where there are
students who don't feel seen

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.410
because they don't have
access to those sort of books

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:10.823
or hear those stories.

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:12.743
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.370
So that was a great
tribute to your teacher.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:19.310
Let's get into this idea of
Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:22.330
You've been very vocal that
there shouldn't even be

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:23.820
a Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:28.133
Tell us why you feel that
way and what your stance is?

00:12:28.133 --> 00:12:31.690
- Well, I mean, I think
everyone should be appreciated.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.520
I mean, we shouldn't have Mother's Day.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:34.950
You shouldn't need a day
to appreciate your mothers,

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:36.490
but we do, we need that reminder.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:37.960
And then I go back to annoying my mom

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.973
the 364 days of the year.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.180
When it comes to professions,

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:44.900
and people have said this for a long time,

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:46.710
if you have an appreciation week,

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.640
you're probably not being appreciated.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.340
And it's kind of that celebration

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.880
or it's everyone likes to be
appreciated on a larger scale.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.450
When it comes to Teacher
Appreciation Week,

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:59.300
I'll be honest, I used to
be, pardon my language,

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:01.480
but I used to be that
toxic positive person

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.480
where you always have to
be positive regardless.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.760
You can't address any negatives.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.100
You can't show any vulnerability.

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:11.060
It always has to be a
positive, positive focus

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:12.680
definitely as an educator.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.750
At the same time you can still be positive

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.240
and be understanding and empathetic

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.810
and also try to push for change.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.420
And when it comes to
Teacher Appreciation Week,

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.020
it's such an interesting thing

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.070
because it made me reevaluate
my whole profession.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:27.610
It also made me reevaluate on

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.890
if teachers are really appreciated?

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:31.297
Which kind of I'll be honest,

00:13:31.297 --> 00:13:34.560
when I was thinking of ways
to amplify teacher stories

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.420
and teacher concerns this year,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.160
one of my thoughts was maybe we should do

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.780
an anti-Teacher Appreciation Week campaign

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:43.840
where it's get rid of
Teacher Appreciation Week

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.690
for other things.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.360
And obviously I kind of
drifted away from that

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.120
because it's still a nice gesture

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.410
and it's still nice to be recognized.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.120
Then I kind of came up with the thought of

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.000
maybe we should just allow
an open-ended question

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.770
of Teacher Appreciation Week is great,

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.740
but what if we really
appreciated teachers?

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:05.130
If we appreciated teachers,
what do we need to change?

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.630
And not that we're not
appreciated, but at the same time,

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:09.590
what we want to change within the system

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.140
and within our professions.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:13.650
So that kind of led me
to push a little campaign

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.040
where I wanted people to
share not only teachers,

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.960
but anybody who's supportive
of public schools and education

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:22.420
and education in general,
to just share what they feel

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.503
needs to change for teachers
to truly be appreciated.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.079
- [Kristen] So tell us
about that, this hashtag,

00:14:30.079 --> 00:14:32.983
#ifweappreciatedteachers, what is that?

00:14:32.983 --> 00:14:34.630
What does it become?

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:36.830
- It's to me, it's awareness.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:39.560
Obviously tweets and posts and hashtags

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.020
don't change society,
don't change culture,

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:43.130
but I always feel like,

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:45.260
and as a history teacher
I've always found that

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.040
change doesn't happen without awareness

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:48.680
until you bring awareness to things.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.410
It's even as a person when I realized,

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.990
became aware of certain
things, it made me more active

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.510
to help change the
things that I can change.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:15:00.340
And something as simple as
#ifweappreciatedteachers

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:03.080
was just an opportunity to
give educators and everyone

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.060
a point to share what they
feel needs to be changed,

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.120
not only so that teachers
feel appreciated,

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:11.320
so that we could be better
teachers for our students,

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.300
we could be better
partners for our spouses,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.390
we could have certain
resources and certain needs met

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:20.540
so we don't continue down
this, I hate to say it,

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:24.820
but this possible struggle
of a teacher attrition

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.880
that will leave schools
without enough educators

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:29.600
to make sure that every student

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.550
has a teacher in front of them.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.450
So I reached out to as
many people as I could,

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:36.450
as many educators, and
there was great feedback

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.240
and great responses,
and just great thoughts.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:42.040
And some of 'em I never
thought about, 'cause again,

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:44.310
being a man, I did not think about...

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.560
I'm aware of paid maternity,
but I never thought about

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.390
how much it affects
the teacher profession,

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:51.210
because we are a profession
predominantly of women.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.920
So it was great to see
everyone's responses.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:57.750
It was hopefully useful in
the way of making people

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.830
more aware of what we're concerned about

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.950
that when we go on social
media, we're not complaining.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:06.270
Me confiding about the stresses of my job

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:07.610
is not complaining about my job,

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.420
it's wanting to be a better person,

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:11.850
so I could be better resourced

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:13.723
and a better teacher for my students.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.360
Teachers, when you help
teachers, you help students.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:18.820
It is not either or.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:19.930
You don't support students,

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.310
and then for you're not
supporting teachers.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.760
Or if you support teachers,
you don't support students.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.500
We are intertwined.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.960
So I just wanted to do something like this

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:31.113
to help bring more
awareness to the concerns,

00:16:31.113 --> 00:16:35.850
not only in America, but just
globally and internationally,

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:37.620
because there's a lot of common concerns

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.410
that teachers are struggling with.

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:40.650
And I just wanna fix it

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.050
before we lose so many amazing educators,

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:45.123
which I'm afraid might happen.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:50.780
- So we saw on the screen
there some of the responses

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:54.770
that teachers and all sorts
of folks were posting.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:55.920
Do you have your own list?

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:59.063
What would your list be if we
really appreciated teachers?

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.550
- I mean, my list is always probably

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:03.400
the most simplistic list.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.420
It's always kind of the core needs

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.790
that I'm in my 19th year as a teacher,

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.580
there's not a single year I
haven't worked an extra job.

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:15.910
There was years I was working
three jobs on top of teaching

00:17:15.910 --> 00:17:18.920
on top of doing my main job.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.060
I always say teaching's one
of the most important careers

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:23.770
that you have to work other careers

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.870
in order to still do your first career.

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:27.380
If you were about to get heart surgery

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:29.340
and you found out your
doctor was driving Uber

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.390
for 12 hours before the heart surgery

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:34.070
you'd be a little suspicious
or cautious or concerned.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:37.230
If you found out your
airline pilot was again,

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.860
working in a Target or
working in a factory

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.380
right before they were
about to fly the plane,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:43.590
you'd be concerned.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.360
I mean, I feel the same
way about teachers.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:48.500
We expect so much and we
give them what's so little.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.790
So obviously the fact that we
don't wanna be millionaires.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.600
It'd be nice if we were,

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:58.020
but just a livable wage
where we can not only live,

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:00.020
but support a family if needed.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:01.570
That's why I always feel like most men

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.690
go into administration
because it pays better.

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:06.250
As a man I could not
support a wife and a child

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:08.030
on my teaching salary,

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:11.003
which is also another problem
which exposes education.

00:18:11.909 --> 00:18:15.720
The fact there's it's
predominantly a female profession

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.800
that we don't value the work
of women as much as we do men.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:21.270
And so the idea of paying
teachers a livable wage.

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.057
You shouldn't be working an
extra job if you're an educator,

00:18:23.057 --> 00:18:25.030
unless it was by choice.

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:26.270
If you want to, that is fine.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.450
It shouldn't be a necessity.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:30.670
The fact that we buy our own supplies

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:32.670
and we beg for supplies online

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:35.260
to me is still one of
the most insane things.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:40.060
I love resources and sites that
allow teachers to crowdfund,

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.590
but at the same time,
we shouldn't have to.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:44.670
If every teacher who
is crowdfunding online

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:47.330
was standing on a street
begging for supplies

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.040
that would be national
news every single day.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.840
So educators shouldn't be expected

00:18:54.431 --> 00:18:57.850
to buy the resources that
they're not provided with.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.840
And we shouldn't be expected to...

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:01.010
At least if we do spend our money,

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:03.470
we should be fully protected.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.200
I'm sorry, the sensor light went out

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.000
which happens in a school,
so I'm gonna move real quick.

00:19:12.256 --> 00:19:14.160
- [Kristen] Sitting in
one position for too long,

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:16.194
I understand-
- I know. I know.

00:19:16.194 --> 00:19:17.027
- [Kristen] in the room.

00:19:17.027 --> 00:19:19.110
- I'm pretty animated, I'm
Italian, my hands should be

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:22.430
enough for these sensors
to keep going, but-

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.330
- I married an Italian and inherited

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.040
some of those hand movements.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.970
- So I get it.
- In my day,

00:19:27.970 --> 00:19:29.585
these lights used to be a little

00:19:29.585 --> 00:19:31.730
more obviously on the sensor side,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.600
but when it comes to the resources,

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:36.750
the fact that it's expected of educators

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:38.847
and the fact we do it and people are like,

00:19:38.847 --> 00:19:41.817
"We should stop doing it, but
we could continue to do it,

00:19:41.817 --> 00:19:45.230
"but fight for the need so
we don't have to do it."

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:48.040
So, and even just something simple.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:49.963
I think the pandemic,

00:19:51.141 --> 00:19:53.710
we went from being
celebrated to being heroes

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:56.037
then to being accused of
just, "Go back to work,

00:19:56.037 --> 00:19:57.700
"we need people to babysit."

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.010
We weren't valued.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.130
We aren't invested in.

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:05.360
And it's I've never seen a
profession so celebrated,

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:10.020
but so demonized or so
undervalued at the same time.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:11.250
And I feel like with educators,

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:14.540
we just want society to invest in us

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:16.330
the same way we invest in our students.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.840
And that's not asking for more,

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.350
that's just asking for enough.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:23.430
So I feel like those three
things from a public opinion,

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.710
the investment, from the resources,

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.590
every classroom should be fully resourced.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:31.200
Every teacher should never
have to come out of pocket.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:33.370
And every student should have teachers

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:35.800
who have all the resources that they need.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.460
And then just for the fact that

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.050
if I only had to work one job,

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:40.620
I would be a better teacher than I am.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.010
And I give my all to teaching already.

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:45.700
So I shouldn't have...

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:47.300
Again, it just, it breaks
my heart to show this,

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:49.550
but my Grubhub driver of weeks ago

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:51.460
he's wearing an NJEA shirt,

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:53.280
which is our union in New Jersey.

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.530
And I asked him, I'm
like, "Are you a teacher?"

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:56.410
And he said, "Yeah. How'd you know?"

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:58.510
And he looked down and we started talking.

00:20:58.510 --> 00:20:59.740
And I was like, "Do you wanna do this?"

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.077
He said, "No, I come right from school.

00:21:01.077 --> 00:21:03.167
"I teach till 4:00, and
then I come right here

00:21:03.167 --> 00:21:04.110
"and do this till 6:00."

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.323
And he's a new father, he has to do it.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:09.610
People should be as angry about that

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.170
as they should anything else?

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:12.970
So just more support.

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:14.300
You don't have to have a child

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.060
to support schools and education.

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:18.470
I think education's the only investment

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:21.220
that really benefits everybody.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:23.320
Outside of universal healthcare,

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:25.200
education is the next best investment

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.763
that benefits everybody.

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:30.630
I'm sorry for that kind of
emotional rollercoaster ride,

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:33.970
but it's just, yeah,
it's like my Uber driver

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:35.540
and talking to him and just seeing that,

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:37.210
it's like it's heartbreaking.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:38.650
And it's and he's not the only one.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:41.680
Majority of a lot of
teachers, a lot of teachers

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:44.063
tend to work extra jobs
just to supplement.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:46.370
- Yeah.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.500
The other thing that I know from teachers

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:53.290
that are in my family
and that I know is that

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:54.950
if they're not working another job,

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.130
they're working their teaching job,

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:01.453
weekends and evenings and
doing all of that work.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.750
How do you feel like we
should address that piece

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:09.070
where there's so much
on the teacher's plates?

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:11.920
- Well, anybody who says
teachers only work days

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.980
and have evenings and
weekends and summers off

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.640
doesn't know any teachers.

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:18.350
And the running joke,
somebody tweeted this.

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:20.927
They're like, "Saying teachers
only teach during school days

00:22:20.927 --> 00:22:22.587
"or work during school days is like saying

00:22:22.587 --> 00:22:25.407
"Tom Brady only works 15 Sundays a year."

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:28.630
Because that's when he actually works

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:30.680
as if he's not working every other day

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:32.720
preparing and everything else.

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:34.390
If we were paid by the hour,

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:35.350
going back to being a millionaire,

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:36.700
we would be millionaires if we were paid

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:40.640
by the actual hour of work we
do because there's so much.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.380
And the irony being,

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:45.440
if I would never encourage
a national strike,

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.510
at the same time part of me feels like

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:52.000
if educators only did
their contractual job,

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:53.710
the education system would fall apart.

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:56.320
If we didn't do the beyond,
the extra hour work,

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:57.720
the working on the weekends,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:58.890
we wouldn't be able to do our job,

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:00.480
but I feel like the education system

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:01.860
would absolutely crumble.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:05.820
It is almost impossible to
fulfill our duties as educators

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.710
within our contractual hours.

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:09.740
We're expected to work outside of them.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:11.970
And if we don't, nothing will get done.

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.230
So we almost feel obligated
to do it because again,

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:15.550
we don't wanna punish our students

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:19.270
because we are not allotted
enough time to get papers,

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.560
lesson planning, professional development

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:22.910
done within the school day.

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:26.030
- So what is the solution for that?

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:29.940
Is it that we need more
teachers so that any individual

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.660
teacher has less of a workload?

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:32.493
Or what is the...

00:23:32.493 --> 00:23:35.023
How do we help fix this?

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:39.200
- Well, I mean, it's no
teachers should have a class,

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:42.950
especially elementary school
of over 15 or 20 students.

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:45.580
I mean, the fact there's
kindergarten teachers with 30 or 40

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.460
absolutely blows my mind.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:50.730
I have high school students
in classes of 30 as well.

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.410
To me, it's impossible
to develop relationships

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.100
with students when you have class sizes...

00:23:57.281 --> 00:23:58.921
I'm sorry.

00:23:58.921 --> 00:24:00.280
Gordon.
- Yes, sir.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:01.730
I'm recording a video.
- Oh, I'm sorry.

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:03.550
- It's all right. Thank you.
- I'll come back.

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:04.724
- All the interruption-
- Students coming in

00:24:04.724 --> 00:24:06.113
all the time.
- (indistinct) students, yep.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:09.553
This is the perfect...

00:24:09.553 --> 00:24:10.860
I just need one announcement

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.750
and one student to walk
in with a late pass,

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:14.230
even though there's no students

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:16.930
just to show you what happens
on a regular school day.

00:24:17.987 --> 00:24:18.843
- That is great.

00:24:19.914 --> 00:24:22.053
- This is not planned, this
is all life of a teacher.

00:24:22.053 --> 00:24:24.943
- This is all life of a
teacher, totally understand.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:28.720
You mentioned, we showed
some of the things

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:29.900
that other teachers have said,

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:31.520
and also you shared your thoughts.

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:34.150
Can people still
participate in the campaign

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:36.883
and share their thoughts about this?

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:39.200
- Absolutely.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:40.823
We have a template, a blank template,

00:24:40.823 --> 00:24:43.350
#ifweappreciatedteachers.

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:46.780
And if you are a parent, if
you're an advocate, if you just,

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:49.600
if everybody has a
teacher in their family,

00:24:49.600 --> 00:24:52.910
just share, fill in the blank,
it's an open-ended question.

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:54.830
Share what you feel needs to happen

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:57.750
to truly let teachers
know we appreciate them.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.430
And the templates, it's very
simple, it's on my socials.

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:03.810
I believe we'll be sharing
it on here as well.

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:04.643
So we'll sharing-
- It's going to be

00:25:04.643 --> 00:25:06.730
in the chats for you that
are watching this, yeah.

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.550
- Yes, it'll be in the chat
for obviously this video.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:12.280
And all you're gonna
do is just make a copy.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:14.930
Very simply insert what
you think needs to happen

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.550
for teachers to be appreciated,
truly to be appreciated.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.520
And then share it and use that hashtag.

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:22.850
And let's just keep awareness.

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:26.410
This conversation can't end
after Teacher Appreciation Week,

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:29.300
because obviously we're
still in the school year.

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:30.910
We'll be back to school next year.

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:33.450
We can't come out of these
past two years without making

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:37.490
some change for the better in
education and for educators.

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:40.670
So all I ask is take three minutes

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:43.270
to think of what you need
think needs to happen,

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.950
and share it, and just kind
of join this little movement

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:47.170
and help bring awareness

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:49.870
to people who may not understand education

00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:51.803
or have connections to teachers.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:54.750
- Great.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:58.550
We have a number of parents
who often engage with us.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:01.410
What kinds of things can parents do

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:04.460
to both support classroom
teaching practices

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:06.610
or in the ways they
talk to their students?

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.160
- Well, I would say, I mean, parents,

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:11.300
my heart goes out to you as well

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.340
because we are in this together.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.540
It is not parents versus teachers.

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.470
We wanna see your child succeed so much.

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:18.990
We wanna work with you.

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:20.410
Which I hate when parents and teachers

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:23.380
tend to get politicized
and put against each other.

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:26.380
But as parents, reach out
to your child's teachers,

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:29.080
and as teachers reach out
to your students' parents,

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:31.830
and just find out what we
can do to work with you.

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:34.830
And as far as supporting
your classroom as a parent,

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:37.450
find out how many students
are in your child's classes.

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:39.530
Find out if the classes
are fully resourced.

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:41.900
If they're not, show up
to those board meetings,

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:46.900
run for school boards, speak
out in defense of the staff.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.280
Put pressure on school boards, I'd say,

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:51.400
but if you put pressure on a school board,

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.230
they will be more inclined
because then they feel like

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:55.660
the community's behind this message.

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:58.450
So be very involved in understanding

00:26:58.450 --> 00:26:59.650
on what's going on in the classroom

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:03.290
as far as if your child's in
a classroom of 35 students,

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:04.820
I'd be concerned with that as a parent.

00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:07.150
I would think my child's not
getting the best education.

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.470
I would then support hiring more teachers

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:12.020
to make sure those
class sizes can go down.

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.900
So just work with teachers,
reach out to teachers.

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:16.740
We are here for you.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.890
We want to do whatever we can from our end

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.730
to not only support your mission,

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:21.620
but to help what you're doing,

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:24.183
but just also to set
your child up to succeed.

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:27.263
- Fantastic.

00:27:27.263 --> 00:27:30.190
So we've talked a lot about
how difficult and challenging

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:31.850
the job of teaching is.

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:33.440
What keeps you inspired?

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:34.740
What keeps you doing this?

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:37.530
- I mean, outside of all the interruptions

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.880
and chaos and dysfunction, it's

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:46.210
we had Miss USA come
by our school on Monday

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:47.920
to surprise our students and staff.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:49.950
And I always use my platform

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:52.590
to get opportunities for my students.

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:56.470
The photographer they hired
to shoot Miss USA's visit

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.620
and videotape it was my former student

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:01.340
who I had as a sophomore in class.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:02.820
My greatest moment as a teacher

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:04.690
was helping him get his
first published photo

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:07.000
on a national magazine which was of me.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:09.070
And as educators

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:11.440
it's teaching is it's

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:13.730
we enjoy nothing more
than seeing our students

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:16.100
go on to be successful
and pursue what they love

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:21.100
and to be happy, successful,
empowered human beings.

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:23.680
I always say a teacher is
someone who dedicated their life

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:26.550
to the success and happiness
of other people's children.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:30.060
But seeing my students
thrive, interacting with them

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:31.810
even days when there's battles.

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:33.549
Every day is not rosy with students.

00:28:33.549 --> 00:28:35.040
They're emotional beings.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:35.990
I'm an emotional being.

00:28:35.990 --> 00:28:37.210
There are days I don't have my coffee.

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:40.100
I'm a little more irritable
than I would be on a normal day.

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.370
So it's just those interactions.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:43.800
And I will say this
with absolute certainty,

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:47.290
and I know 99.9% of teachers
would agree with me.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:50.320
If a teacher ever leaves
teaching and a lot of 'em are,

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:52.100
it is not because of the students.

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:54.890
That is the only reason
teachers will endure

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:57.560
what we're enduring and what
we've been bearing for so long

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.690
is because we do it for our students.

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:01.700
We did not become teachers for the salary,

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:04.400
the fame or the summers off.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.760
But the fact that it's
interesting enough where

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.330
for some of us it's our dream job.

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:11.820
The fact that we have people
leaving their dream job

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:14.520
should tell a lot of people how unbearable

00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:17.830
some situations have become.

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:20.340
But it's my students,
it's that interaction,

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:21.500
they keep me inspired.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:22.730
I'm a better person.

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:25.160
I would not be half the person I am today

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:28.820
if not for having new groups
of students every year

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.300
who hold me to a higher
standard, who better me.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:33.300
It's like that makes me a better person.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:36.270
- Fantastic.

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:39.530
It has been a pleasure
to talk to you today.

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:41.530
If folks want to hear more from you

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.477
or find out what else
are you up to coming up,

00:29:44.477 --> 00:29:46.160
what do you have coming up?

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:48.110
- You could follow me on my socials,

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.680
Instagram and Twitter is @NicholasFerroni.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:53.160
I am also collaborating with Kahoot.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.290
I'm hosting a little
live game show for them

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:56.620
every two weeks.

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:58.660
Our first Kahoot game show is tomorrow.

00:29:58.660 --> 00:29:59.840
And it's not only for teachers,

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:04.080
it's for teachers, students,
game show enthusiasts.

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.270
Our first one's tomorrow
at 3:00 PM Eastern Time

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:07.970
at Kahoot's Instagram.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.350
So join us, it'll be a lot of fun,

00:30:10.350 --> 00:30:13.320
but just feel free to message me, DM me,

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:15.920
or reach out if there's
any way I could help.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:17.480
Especially as educators.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:19.629
I love sharing the stories
of amazing teachers

00:30:19.629 --> 00:30:21.993
and I feel like not enough
people hear those stories.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:24.353
- Fantastic.

00:30:24.353 --> 00:30:25.610
Thanks so much for your time today.

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:29.143
Thanks everyone who joined us
and we will see you next time.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:32.810
- [Nicholas] Thank you so much.

